5.text:
Gragas, Vi, Jarvan, Fizz, LeBlanc, Galio, Poppy, Pyke, Riven, Yone, Urgot, Renekton, Nidalee, Fiora, Camille

Core unanimous dash-Flash set is Gragas E Rolling Corkscrew, Vi Q Vi’s Vault, Jarvan E Dragon Strike, Fizz E Playful Trickster, Yone Q3 and Camille E Hookshot. These all have a travel / wind-up window that can be buffered into Flash to cancel the animation and land the dash’s own damage/CC on a different target point.

On the disputed names the strict damage/CC-on-landing criterion is met by:
LeBlanc W Distortion – the return leg has a travel window and the mark/damage is on the arrival/return;
Galio E Shield of Durand – a travel dash that lands the knock-up;
Poppy E Heroic Charge – stun on arrival is the purpose of the buffer;
Pyke E Bone Skewer – the skewer travel and the impact damage/root can be extended with Flash;
Riven Q3 – empowered forward dash with damage and the classic Flash-Q surprise;
Urgot E Noxian Flesh? Slide Charge – dash with damage and root on arrival;
Renekton E Slice and Dice – damage-carrying lunge that is commonly flash-buffered;
Nidalee W Pouncing Paws – damage/slow on arrival lunge;
Fiora Q Lunge – damage-carrying lunge.

Champions excluded under the stated note are the pure reposition/teleport cases and non-dash abilities: Shen E Shadow Dash is ally teleport reposition only, Sejuani Q Winter’s Wrath is a skillshot stun with no dash, Corki W is not a dash, and Rakan W, Rell W, Zac E, Kayn Q, Ornn E, Bel’Veth Q, Tryndamere E, Tristana W are either ally-reposition/ follow-up focused or lack a consistent, damage/CC-on-landing buffer window in current patch behaviour.

